ld/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "417e9b86-46f1-5505-b2b8-827b75bf916b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455013} {:answer/id #uuid "467d0a11-3d59-5056-8c67-302264331708" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455021} {:answer/id #uuid "4d50a330-e973-52eb-b27c-39dd3a1bb1d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455038} {:answer/id #uuid "5fcb936a-b5c3-5867-a96a-a7f463d31cb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455077}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d50a330-e973-52eb-b27c-39dd3a1bb1d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455038} :answer-field/id #uuid "8703dd91-2500-56b8-9fc9-0a71b2701f5c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455381}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "58703cdd-6a5b-5d3e-93ac-66e311d20a8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-344278" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2y=f(x)\n$$\n\non $[-4,4]$, where $f$ is $8$-periodic and even. Which Fourier-series form is appropriate for a particular solution ${y}_{p}?$\n\n$\\,$\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Because $f$ is $8$-periodic on $[-4,4]$, we have $L=4$, so Fourier series on this interval use the basis\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4})\\,\\text{and}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4}).\n$$\n\nNext, since $f$ is even, its Fourier series contains only cosine terms. For a second-order equation with no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits this property and is also an even function.\n\nTherefore, the appropriate form for ${y}_{p}$ is a cosine series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4})\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391884 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231405 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b3a1dcb-3788-58c3-9bf2-b0f681060ffd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Form of the Fourier Series For the Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10c02dac-dc9a-58f5-ac61-be40e997ed00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391886} {:answer/id #uuid "a756987e-8fb9-5f04-9834-f95885d2ee05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391887} {:answer/id #uuid "2d3e1e17-92c5-52da-b301-a2ebd8bda2b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391888} {:answer/id #uuid "50a798b3-b743-56ed-9b8e-f33b96e9fcb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391889}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d3e1e17-92c5-52da-b301-a2ebd8bda2b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391888} :answer-field/id #uuid "9885c458-8f5d-5130-8d86-c8da5b1b3800" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391885}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e1231bd2-0ea0-512f-a509-a32953caed3d" :question/math-academy-id "q-344280" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{2}\\,y=f(x)\n$$\n\non $[-2,2]$, where $f$ is $4$-periodic and even. Which Fourier-series form is appropriate for a particular solution ${y}_{p}?$\n\n$\\,$\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Because $f$ is $4$-periodic on $[-2,2]$, we have $L=2$, so Fourier series on this interval use the basis\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})\\,\\text{and}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nNext, since $f$ is even, its Fourier series contains only cosine terms. For a second-order equation with no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits this property and is also an even function.\n\nTherefore, the appropriate form for ${y}_{p}$ is a cosine series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497356 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231405 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b3a1dcb-3788-58c3-9bf2-b0f681060ffd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Form of the Fourier Series For the Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03bcde23-8fb4-54be-b4f9-12b3458b95b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2n\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496665} {:answer/id #uuid "0d8a9980-7371-5df1-8410-26fa8e6ff9c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496683} {:answer/id #uuid "1989b09c-b976-5029-b925-43a8bcf85f00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2n\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496712} {:answer/id #uuid "86a2fd99-6f60-54d4-ba6c-934c44c7e141" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496935}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1989b09c-b976-5029-b925-43a8bcf85f00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2n\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496712} :answer-field/id #uuid "48d46346-4c74-57e4-aaa3-613d6a4a40ae" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497248}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4f217825-c491-59e7-9325-ea7e5e5d43ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-344285" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=f(x)\n$$\n\non $[-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{3}{2}]$, where $f$ is $3$-periodic and even. Which Fourier-series form is appropriate for a particular solution ${y}_{p}?$\n\n$\\,$\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Because $f$ is $3$-periodic on $[-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{3}{2}]$, we have $L=\\frac{3}{2}$, so Fourier series on this interval use the basis\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2n\\pi x}{3})\\,\\text{and}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNext, since $f$ is even, its Fourier series contains only cosine terms. For a second-order equation with no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits this property and is also an even function.\n\nTherefore, the appropriate form for ${y}_{p}$ is a cosine series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2n\\pi x}{3})\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455483 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231406 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee5c94d6-9a8e-5ab4-ba71-de5ce850f903" :knowledge-point/title "Solving BVPs Using Fourier Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30fa23ce-2a64-5f21-af68-65dc34a26e11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{10})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{10}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454978} {:answer/id #uuid "966b9893-02ce-5002-92b6-528d0cbf1845" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{10})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{10}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455174} {:answer/id #uuid "cd4cbb92-f7b5-5cd0-b586-5f6f713d2615" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{10})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{10}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455258} {:answer/id #uuid "cea66f81-9d52-5528-86a7-49bf7d5e5e3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{10})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{10}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455264}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cea66f81-9d52-5528-86a7-49bf7d5e5e3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{10})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{10}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455264} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0337040-d506-5af7-a704-b40619be2791" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455419}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9a34abd9-b695-5ab7-980c-61ca9112523d" :question/math-academy-id "q-344326" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+10y=f(x),\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=6\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is $4$-periodic and odd. Select the missing part in the solution of the BVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}^{′}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}(1)=0.$*\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write the solution as $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}$, where ${y}_{c}$ solves the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, solve the homogeneous equation ${y}^{″}+10y=0.$ Its general solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{10}x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{10}x).\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=-A\\sqrt{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{10}x)+B\\sqrt{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{10}x)+{y}_{p}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we use the hint. Since ${y}_{p}^{′}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}(1)=0$, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(0)={y}_{c}^{′}(0)\\,\\text{and}\\,{y}^{′}(1)={y}_{c}^{′}(1).\n$$\n\nNext, we apply the boundary conditions. \n\n- Use ${y}^{′}(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =0+B\\sqrt{10}(1)+{y}_{p}^{′}(0) \\\\ 0 & =B\\sqrt{10}+0 \\\\ B & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{10}x).$\n\n- Use ${y}^{′}(1)=6$ with $B=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(1) & =-A\\sqrt{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{10})+{y}_{p}^{′}(1) \\\\ 6 & =-A\\sqrt{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{10})+0 \\\\ A & =-\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{10})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼-\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{10})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{10}x)+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$"}]}
